            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Lead with the Conclusion: Cold Launch Relies on a Material Pool, Not a Single "Viral" Ad</h2>
<p>Many teams launching new products on TikTok Shop tend to spend excessive time crafting one "perfect" ad, hoping it will become an overnight hit. In the algorithm-driven feed environment, this approach has extremely low success rates.</p>
<p>The truly effective cold launch strategy is <strong>building a material pool</strong>: testing 15-30 materials with different angles and selling points simultaneously, quickly identifying high-performing ads through data feedback, then concentrating budget on scaling. The core logic is—before the algorithm identifies your target audience, you have no way of knowing which ad will trigger its recommendation mechanism, so you trade quantity for probability.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A utility app team in Southeast Asia launched with 22 materials in their first week, concentrating 80% of their budget on the top 3 performers. The result was a 2.3x improvement in ROI compared to single-material testing approaches.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Three Core Dimensions of a Material Pool</h2>
<h3>1. Segmented Selling Points: Don't Put All Eggs in One Basket</h3>
<p>The biggest risk during cold launch is "betting on the wrong selling point." Users' perception of a new product is vague, and your core selling point may not be what they actually value.</p>
<p>We recommend dividing materials into three layers:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Functional</strong>: Highlight what problem the product solves—"saves 50% time" or "one-click solution"</li>
<li><strong>Emotional</strong>: Emphasize usage scenarios and emotional value—"perfect gift" or "ultimate ambiance"</li>
<li><strong>Comparative</strong>: Use before/after comparisons, unboxing, or competitor comparisons to create impact</li>
</ul>
<p>Prepare at least 3-5 materials for each layer. Don't subjectively judge which is most effective—let the data speak.</p>
<h3>2. Format Diversity: Rotate Through Landscape, Vertical, Spoken, and Graphic Formats</h3>
<p>TikTok's traffic distribution has format preferences that are constantly evolving. During cold launch, ensure your material formats cover:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Format</th>
<th>Best Use Case</th>
<th>Production Cost</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Native vertical video</td>
<td>Showcase authentic usage scenarios</td>
<td>Medium</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Spoken review/testimonial</td>
<td>Build trust for functional products</td>
<td>Lower</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Image carousel</td>
<td>Dense information, tutorial content</td>
<td>Lower</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Scenario short drama</td>
<td>Strong product seeding, but longer production cycle</td>
<td>Higher</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Initially focus on low-cost, high-volume formats (e.g., real footage + voiceover) to quickly accumulate testing data.</p>
<h3>3. Regional Adaptation: Avoid One-Size-Fits-All Localization</h3>
<p>TikTok Shop's core markets (Southeast Asia, UK, US) have vastly different user profiles. The same "multi-functional storage box" might emphasize "space-saving" in Southeast Asia but "ins-style home décor" appeal in the UK.</p>
<p>Prepare at least 2-3 localized material versions for each product, adapting language, subtitles, music, and even subtitle font colors to match local aesthetic preferences.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Cadence for Material Testing During Cold Launch</h2>
<p>The cold launch period typically spans 7-14 days, with the following cadence:</p>
<p><strong>Days 1-3</strong>: Batch-upload materials, allocating equal small budgets to each (recommend $50-100/day/material). Monitor impressions and click-through rates.</p>
<p><strong>Days 4-7</strong>: Disable materials with CTR below 1%, concentrating budget on the top 3-5 performers. At this stage, minor adjustments to thumbnails or the opening 3 seconds can improve retention.</p>
<p><strong>Days 8-14</strong>: Allocate 70% of budget to scaling materials, 20% to continued testing of promising candidates, and 10% reserved for exploring new directions. If a high-performing material's CPM suddenly drops, it may be experiencing creative fatigue—promptly supplement with fresh materials.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Pitfall Avoidance: Common Cold Launch Mistakes</h2>
<h3>Testing Only One Material, Missing the Golden Window</h3>
<p>Many teams believe "one test is enough." When results are poor, they write off the product. However, TikTok's algorithm has inherent randomness—the same material can perform very differently at different times or with varying account weights. Without sufficient quantity, conclusions remain unreliable.</p>
<h3>No Iteration After Launch</h3>
<p>A material pool isn't "set and forget." High-performing materials also decline. Review material data every 3-5 days, promptly replacing declining creatives to keep the pool fresh.</p>
<h3>Ignoring Compliance and Platform Policies</h3>
<p>TikTok Shop has strict restrictions on efficacy claims for beauty and health products. Terms like "highly effective" or "instant results" can trigger throttling or even account suspension. During cold launch, avoid provocative borderline expressions—account权重 recovery costs are very high once damaged.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Bottom Line</h2>
<p>The core of TikTok Shop cold launch is replacing single materials with a material pool, and replacing subjective betting with systematic testing. Segmented selling points, format diversity, and regional adaptation form the three pillars of building a material pool. Reasonable testing cadence and timely iteration are key to keeping the material pool productive.</p>
<p>Remember: In an algorithmic environment, the best way to reduce trial-and-error costs is to give the machine enough material options to choose from.</p>
